{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-15 06:47:50 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-15 13:47:50 +07\n\nThe strongest opportunities are in PHP and Python’s site-set infrastructure, followed by the identical balanced prelude emitter in PHP and TypeScript. The estimates below are approximate **net source-line savings**, after allowing for a shared helper and target-specific calls; they are not estimates of guest runtime code removed.\n\nThe audit was read-only. Similarity claims below are based on direct comparison of the cited regions. No tests were run because no code was changed.\n\n## Compiler surface\n\nThe requested paths differ slightly from this checkout:\n\n- PHP, Python, and TypeScript have ANTLR compiler trees under `src/cljc/yang/{php,python,typescript}/antlr/`. Their `lower.cljc` files are approximately 4,113, 2,375, and 2,402 lines respectively. Their main preludes are approximately 6,360, 3,820, and 4,439 lines.\n- Clojure has no `clojure/antlr/` tree here. Its compiler is [yang/clojure.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc), approximately 3,121 lines, with a 248-line [prelude](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc).\n- `yang/core` and `yang/antlr` are directories, not `core.cljc` and `antlr.cljc`. Existing shared machinery includes [core/lower.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/core/lower.cljc), [core/prelude.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc), [core/publication.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc), [core/stage.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/core/stage.cljc), and [antlr/packet.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/antlr/packet.cljc). The other shared roots are [frontend.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/frontend.cljc), [stage.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/stage.cljc), [safepoint.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc), and [tails.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc).\n- PHP’s [prelude_p9.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:1) supplies string, array, JSON, and math builtin guest definitions; [prelude_module.cljc](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:1) supplies include/require guest definitions. They are extensions, **not near-duplicates** of the main prelude: [prelude.cljc:6188](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:6188) concatenates their definition vectors with its core definitions before initialization.\n\n## Ranked opportunities\n\n1. **Share site-set tree loading and buffered writes.** PHP [siteset.cljc:166](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:166) and Python [siteset.cljc:310](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:310) have identical `load-tree` bodies, approximately 27 lines each. Their `overlay` bodies at [PHP:195](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:195) and [Python:459](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:459) are also identical, approximately 33 lines each. For example, both traverse the semantic-bytecode grammar with the same `:node`/`:nodes` cases, and both buffer bytes in insertion order before `flush!`.\n\n   Put these in a shared namespace such as `yang.core.siteset-store`; pass the address reader and its absence predicate into tree loading, or move the reader and sentinel with it. **Estimated saving: 50–60 lines.** Risk is low if the `::absent` sentinel’s namespace, first-missing traversal order, duplicate-write behavior, and the `#?(:cljs … :default …)` sentinel construction remain unchanged. The overlay’s staged mutation is explicit and should remain scoped to a single derivation.\n\n2. **Share balanced definition-to-UAST emission.** [PHP prelude:6197](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:6197) and [TypeScript prelude:4306](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:4306) contain the same approximately 14-line balanced `sequence-tree` implementation. Both map `[key form]` to `u/def!` and split the resulting vector at its midpoint. A pure helper in `yang.core.prelude` could serve both. **Estimated saving: 10–15 lines.**\n\n   Preserve the exact tree shape, left-to-right effects, last-value behavior, and empty-input behavior: a different tree can change content addresses. Python’s [prelude:3684](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3684) instead uses `u/seq-nodes`; do not silently switch it to balancing. Its shape and addresses need separate review.\n\n3. **Share site-publication envelope construction.** The bodies of `signed-entry` and `entry-datoms` are identical in [PHP siteset:81](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:81) and [Python siteset:144](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:144), about 17 lines per target. `signed-entries` is also the same map-and-sort operation at [PHP:125](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:125) and [Python:541](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:541). Move those pure operations to `yang.core.siteset-publication`, retaining public aliases if needed. **Estimated saving: 20–30 lines.**\n\n   Do **not** unify discovery unchanged: PHP [siteset:115](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:115) requires the envelope’s asserted principal to match the verifying key; Python [siteset:185](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:185) checks the signature against declared keys without that additional comparison. A shared discovery helper would need an explicit policy seam and tests of both behaviors.\n\n4. **Share the common safepoint definition emitter.** PHP [safepoint:40](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:40), Python [safepoint:361](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:361), and TypeScript [safepoint:119](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/safepoint.cljc:119) use the same `u/seq-nodes` conversion from definition pairs. Python’s prelude uses that same conversion too. A named helper in `yang.core.prelude` would remove repeated mechanics. **Estimated saving: 5–10 lines**, modest but sound if it keeps `u/seq-nodes`’ exact shape.\n\n   The larger Python/TypeScript safepoint module emitters are already mostly calls into `yang.core.prelude`. Their [Python:386](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:386) and [TypeScript:143](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/safepoint.cljc:143) `linked-program` functions look alike but differ in require order: Python explicitly requires `yang.call`, then its prelude and hook module; TypeScript does not. TypeScript also annotates its module tree at [safepoint:189](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/safepoint.cljc:189). A larger factor would have to expose those differences, for little additional saving.\n\n5. **Share small literal and UAST helpers selectively.** Python [lower:344](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:344) and TypeScript [lower:243](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:243) have identical `code-point->str` functions, including all three host reader branches. Their `build-vector` functions at [Python:234](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:234) and [TypeScript:177](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:177) differ only in the emitted `py/vconj` versus `js/vconj` symbol. `yang.core.lower` could expose the former directly and the latter with that symbol as data. **Estimated saving: 5–10 lines.**\n\n   Keep validation and diagnostics in the callers. For example, Python’s [hex-value](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:362) checks the Unicode maximum and emits its own error; TypeScript’s [hex-value](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:302) has different diagnostics and bounds handling.\n\n## Similar code I would leave target-specific\n\n- **Site admission and derivation:** PHP [siteset:58](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:58) treats an empty set as a valid publication and verifies canonical set ordering; Python [siteset:219](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:219) reports detailed defects and rejects an empty set. Their closure walks at [PHP:256](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc:256) and [Python:414](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:414) both sort dependencies and derive bottom-up, but differ in admission, missing/refusal propagation, runtime requirements, instrumentation, primitive discovery, and derivation records. A generic traversal is possible, but its policy surface would be substantial; the isolated identical helpers above are safer.\n\n- **Guest preludes:** The large definition vectors encode each language’s runtime semantics, not just host-language emission. PHP uses `defquoted`; its [macro](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/quoted.cljc:8) emits an EDN read on ClojureDart to avoid compiling quoted data into excessive straight-line Dart. Changing representation, definition order, or UAST shape can affect semantics, host compilation, and content addresses. Share emitter mechanics, not guest definitions merely because their operations have similar names.\n\n- **Rendering:** Python [render:38](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/render.cljc:38) and TypeScript [render:38](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/render.cljc:38) both format shortest float digits, but the diff shows different fixed/exponent thresholds, exponent formatting, special values, and negative-zero handling. A configurable formatter would save little while making language conformance harder to inspect.\n\n- **Scope and lowering:** Similar function names do not establish equivalent semantics. PHP’s [scope walker](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/scope.cljc:147), Python’s [collector and scope analysis](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/scope.cljc:188), and TypeScript’s [analysis](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/scope.cljc:236) reflect different grammars and binding rules. Likewise, [PHP’s `wrap-escape`](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:2323) consults a frame’s used escapes, whereas [TypeScript’s](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:233) receives an explicit boolean and symbol. Existing `yang.core.lower` helpers already capture several common primitives; a broad common walker or lowerer is not justified by these similarities.\n\n- **Publication wrappers:** PHP [publication:26](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc:26) and Python [publication:28](/tmp/yang-audit-wt/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/publication.cljc:28) already delegate ordering and publishing to `yang.core.publication`. Their remaining configuration captures real differences, including PHP class-map and alias handling and Python’s pinned `yang.call` address.\n\nVERDICT: Factor the identical PHP/Python site-set tree loader and buffered store overlay first, preserving missing-address and write-order behavior."}}
